WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:00.620
Hi

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.660
My name is Chris Gagné and I'm an Agile coach

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.840
Today, I will talk about the Definition of Done

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:12.300
The Definition of Done is a documented team agreement

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.360
It defines the conditions that must be met

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.200
For a potentially shippable product to be considered

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:19.520
Done as in done

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.520
It's how we know that we did the thing right

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.120
Meaning we built in the correct level of quality into the product

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.340
These are different from the acceptance criteria which are written by the product owner

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.020
And help us know that we did the right thing

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.760
Creating and following the Definition of Done diligently

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.980
Can easily double the speed of an Agile team

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.560
Lean practitioners will tell you that in the long run

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.080
Quality is free!

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.200
Because if you don't build a quality product in the short run

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.800
You're going to pay for it in the long run due to increased technical debt

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.920
The slow pace of manual testing

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.460
And production bugs

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.220
Having lots of hidden undone work in your product

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.820
Will make releasing your feature to customers

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.900
Difficult and risky

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.340
Scrum doesn't talk about this much detail

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.160
But many less mature teams use two sets of Definition of Done

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.180
One is targeted at what you will consider done

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.440
For a single product increment that you are bundling into a release

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.220
The other is the Definition of Done for that entire release

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:28.140
Over time you want to improve your practices

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:29.260
Environment

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:30.700
And team structure

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.100
So that you can potentially release any given product increment at any time

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:38.600
So what should you include in your Definition of Done?

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.900
Well at a minimum, you're going to want your work to be

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.260
Code complete

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.040
Unit, integration, and performance tested

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:50.160
You want to have just enough documentation

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.980
No new bugs or technical debt

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.500
And lastly accepted by the product owner who is meeting the acceptance criteria

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.600
I suggest spending an hour as a team to review sample Definitions of Done

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:03.960
And craft your own

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:08.800
Then review your Definition of Done periodically, so that it fits your team's retrospectives

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.380
At the beginning, your Definition of Done may have just a few items

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.180
And as your team

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:16.940
And automation matures

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.580
Expand your Definition of Done

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.420
To include more scope

